01:11:52.240 --> 01:11:54.760
Bad interviewer in my case or something.

01:11:54.900 --> 01:11:56.400
Because you said this really profound thing.

01:11:56.600 --> 01:12:03.440
You said that when you went with Tyler to the Vatican Observatory, they knew who he was before having gone there.

01:12:03.760 --> 01:12:07.120
And that tripped me out when I was watching it over in the edit.

01:12:07.260 --> 01:12:12.500
Because I was like, why does the Vatican Observatory, they're obviously very well respected and well staffed.

01:12:12.500 --> 01:12:13.700
When it comes to real astronomy.

01:12:14.200 --> 01:12:18.280
But how do they know who a NASA mission controller from the U.S. is?

01:12:18.940 --> 01:12:19.340
Yeah.

01:12:19.460 --> 01:12:20.320
Not only that.

01:12:20.320 --> 01:12:31.260
But when we were there, we just happened to correspond with a trip that all these young scientists from ESA, so the European Space Agency, they were all there, too.

01:12:31.760 --> 01:12:41.260
And so Brother Guy, who's the director, Guy Consolmagno, he was giving them, you know, a really awesome tour and talk and everything.

01:12:41.520 --> 01:12:42.320
So he introduced us.

01:12:42.500 --> 01:12:44.320
And so he introduced me.

01:12:44.540 --> 01:12:47.700
And they were like, hmm, you know, kind of cool, clap, clap, clap.

01:12:47.920 --> 01:12:50.620
And they introduced Tyler with his real name.

01:12:51.140 --> 01:12:56.380
And the whole place just erupted into just, yes, like that.

01:12:56.600 --> 01:13:01.700
And that was a recognition for me that Americans kind of don't know what's going on.

01:13:01.820 --> 01:13:02.660
But they did.

01:13:02.760 --> 01:13:06.060
Yeah, they were, to them, he was a hero.

01:13:06.780 --> 01:13:07.040
Yeah.

01:13:07.240 --> 01:13:08.580
It was really, really cool.

01:13:08.820 --> 01:13:09.640
That's incredible.

